 happened legislatively in Florida and  then also at the national level one  thing has happened in Florida and that  was positive we got a bill signed and  that was good that's not gonna stop  there though because it's you know it's  just the beginning it's it was barely  anything it was important but as Jackie  said in 60 minutes it would not have  stopped what had happened at our school  were fighting for that change I think  what's important to realize with that  law too is there are a lot of loopholes  in it you can be 18 and buy a gun  privately still under that law and  a lot of gun purchases are purchased  that way and they it doesn't take care  of the gun show loophole it doesn't  require universal background check that  has 97% support among constituents it  doesn't cover so many basic things that  have a widespread support on both sides  of the aisle and that's the part that I  hate most about you know one thing that  stood out from your interview with 60  minutes was you called yourself the mass  shooting generation and you know you  think about the post 9/11 generation  right you really put it into context  what you a student's experience how has  that impacted you even before that that  deadly shooting how has that impacted  you on a day-to-day basis I think on a  day-to-day basis the fact that we even  have to talk about having active shooter  drills it's distracting from our school  life it's distracting from us just being  trying to be American kids and live our  own lives because they're turning our  schools into war zones and when we add  guns there I think we're just gonna make  it even worse even after we included all  this new legislation we required  training for all these teachers we  required psychological checks to make  sure that they're okay we made sure that  they were well trained and had  background checks why not just do that  for every single person I just want to  point out as a young woman it always  feels like you're living in a war zone  there's there's nothing that you can't  be oblivious to walking around in the  world no ma you wrote something very  moving in Harper's Bazaar you said my  name is Emma Gonzales I'm 18 years old  Cuban and bisexual I'm so indecisive  that I can't pick a favorite color I'm  allergic to 12 things but none of this  matters anymore  were the two of you involved in student  government before this happened no we  were science buddies to be honest we're  kind of we're kind of the Nerds of the  school I we're the last people that I  would ever expect well at least I am  Emma's always been an amazing person and  I think I even called the day before the  shooting not just to say because I just  finished up the documentary that's been  too much working on and she's played a  heavy role tonight and I realized really  what an amazing and dedicated person she  is and I just called her to tell her I  didn't know what she was going to do  next what I knew was going to change the  world the two of you along with others  became leaders of this movement did you  all get together did you all talk about  it immediately what happens so here's  what happened there I got a I got a text  that like well we're you know the day  the rally right yeah David's like these  guys are gonna take us out to dinner I'm  like all right so they're like we're  gonna pick up Cameron on the way and I'm  like cool Cameron kaske I know him he  was in Little Shop of Horrors and we  don't pick him up  he's not wearing pants he was like give  me one second and that was pretty much  that's the entire relationship with  Cameron are there it's like usually none  of us are usually wearing the right  amount clothes you're talking about  which role are you talking about him was  a rally where I gave the speech okay and  but but I mean but how did it come about  though that shortly after the shooting  that the two of you really became a  voice of this that's what I'm talking  about and very early on you had that  videotape yeah I think the way that we  really came about as a voice initially  the even the day of the shooting I I  went on news and did a lot  I did a hit with five million people  viewing live and then the next within  the next couple of days I was supposed  to be on the Anderson but the night I  was supposed to be on in again and they  didn't want me on there twice so I  called em I was like hey do you want to  go on there and that's where she did her  first live interview and what's the most  encouraging thing any politician has  said to you thank you actually I've just  been pretty cool and you know they're  throw up and some people on Twitter who  are like you know keep keep fighting out  there but they're not actually I don't  know the I know that the wheels of  bureaucracy turned very slowly but  they're not actually doing anything at  least that's what it feels like and I  feel like everybody needs to try harder  like politicians need to step up their  act you wrote and wrote and report kids  are acting like adults and adults are  acting like kids because kids have had  become a dirty word like you're acting  like children  that's offensive is to be called a child  and in fact we've been raised in this  school environment to reach it to show  what an adult should be we're being  raised and we're being pushed into the  real world so quickly we're never  learned how to balance a checkbook we  learn how to balance a checkbook in  fifth grade we're expected to know all  this stuff when we leave high school  just in case we don't make it to college  I think what's important to realize here  is that the one thing that the NRA is  trying to push right now is they're  trying to make sure that we forget about  this they expect our generation to have  a short attention span if we are not  going to let that happen because the  second that we forget about this it can  and it will be you which is one of the  reasons why you delayed the rally the  nationwide rally for  this coming up Saturday we've heard your  voices loud and clear what is your  message to other high school students  across the country who also are part of  the mass shooting generation I came out  there and vote yeah our message is make  sure that you get out there and vote  make sure that you are registered to  vote so you can vote in primaries and  make sure that you stay educated for the  rest of your life because the seeds of  corruption and greed are always being  sowed it's our duty as a democracy to  make sure that they don't spread you're  both getting death threats does that  deter you no I don't not at all I'm  gonna gets death threats at some point  in their life I feel be it from stalker  that's how we knew what we're doing  matters yeah people want to stop us  they're attacking us personally because  they can't find fault in our message  because from every single angle the  everybody knows on a certain level that  this is unacceptable and it was never it  should never have happened like for 20  years it should never have happened a  lot of people are listening to what you  all have to say  Emma Gonzalez David hog thank you very  much for coming to the table this  morning and then David as you know are  among the student activists featured in  the new CBS primetime documentary it's  called 39 days CBS News embedded with  the students in the weeks after the  Stoneman Douglas shooting as they turn  their grief into action the march for  our lives in Washington will take place  39 days after the tragedy and you can  watch 39 days Saturday in a Saturday  night at 9:00 8:00 central right here on  CBS

